      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A series of nanomolar phosphonate matrix metalloproteinase (MPP) inhibitors was tested for inhibitory activity against a panel of selected human carbonic anhydrase (CA, EC 4.2.1.1) isozymes, covering the cancer-associated CA IX and XII. None of the reported sulfonyl and sulfonylamino-derivatives sensitively affected the catalytic activity of the cytosolic isoforms CA I and II, which are considered off-target isoforms in view of their physiological role. The most active inhibitors were in the series of chiral N-(sulfonyl)phosphovaline derivatives, which showed good to excellent inhibitory activity over target CAs, with compound 15 presenting the best isoform-selectivity toward CA IX. We suggest here that the phosphonates have the potential as dual inhibitors of MMPs and CAs, both involved in tumor formation, invasion and metastasis."},
